    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fault permeability can differ greatly from the permeability of the pristine rock, leading to reservoir compartmentalization if the faults are impermeable. Fault-controlled hydrocarbon migration is likely if fault permeability is high. However,as we know that secondary migration occurs preferentially in coarse-grained, porous sand units, fault permeability is often ignored, and hydrocarbon migration is treated as if flow occurs where sands overlap while shales juxtaposed against sands are treated as seals.\r\n\r\nHere we present numerical results, obtained with a new finite-element multigrid technique, that show how fluid flow patterns at fault-sand intersections vary with fault permeability. Flows calculated in cross-sectional models of a faulted sand-shale sequences how that the fault permeability, as well as the juxtaposition of sands across the fault, exert a decisive control on the patterns of flow. The more the permeability of the fault and that of the intersected rock differ, the more the flow patterns diverge from simple migration across overlapping sands. Highly permeable faults could control secondary migration in sands up to a distance from the fault of several times the fault length. This greatly exceeds the maximum flow-focusing distance that is predicted for faults in isotropic rocks.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
